# Optional credentials for the pre-declared AI Gateway policies in
# config/policies.json. Only required when an application's policy chain
# selects the matching policy. Configure these as environment variables on
# your Zuplo project (Settings -> Environment Variables) for deployed
# environments.

# Used by generic-jwt-auth-inbound (OpenIdJwtInboundPolicy). Point these at
# any OIDC-compliant IDP (Okta, Auth0, Zuplo Auth, etc.) for production.
#
# To test locally against this repo's own demo IDP endpoints instead of a
# real IDP, use the values below and mint a token with:
#   node scripts/mint-demo-jwt.mjs --sub=user-123
JWT_ISSUER=http://localhost:9000
JWT_AUDIENCE=generic-ai-gateway
JWT_JWKS_URL=http://localhost:9000/.well-known/jwks.json

# For a DEPLOYED gateway, JWT_JWKS_URL must NOT point back at this same
# gateway's own domain: Cloudflare Workers can't make an outbound fetch back
# to their own zone (it times out with a 522), so
# OpenIdJwtInboundPolicy's JWKS fetch would always fail. Point it at the
# static copy served from GitHub instead (config/demo-jwks.json), and keep
# JWT_ISSUER as your deployed gateway's own URL (issuer is just an
# identifier string, it doesn't need to be fetchable):
#   JWT_ISSUER=https://<your-deployed-gateway>.zuplo.app
#   JWT_AUDIENCE=generic-ai-gateway
#   JWT_JWKS_URL=https://raw.githubusercontent.com/<org>/<repo>/main/config/demo-jwks.json
#   node scripts/mint-demo-jwt.mjs --sub=user-123 --issuer=https://<your-deployed-gateway>.zuplo.app
#
# A real IDP (Okta, Auth0, etc.) doesn't have this problem since its JWKS
# lives on a different domain than your gateway.

